      "Every stored int16 value in [-32768,32767] is legal; largest |result| <= N*32768 <= 4194304 fits int32, so exact integer equality (atol=0, rtol=0) is required \u2014 no tolerance.",
      "Output: fresh int32 tensor of shape [B,N] on x.device; input must be preserved; coefficient ordering (sequency) is part of correctness.",
      "Any algorithm satisfying the target over the entire legal domain is acceptable; correctness is a single verdict over the whole domain, not per-input.",
      "reference() independently builds H_N, sorts rows by sign-change count, and multiplies in int64 before exact int32 conversion; validate_inputs() enforces the domain and is called by run()."
    ],
    "kernel_model": [
      "Triton kernel _transform_kernel (kernel.py lines 11\u201328): one program per row; loads the full row as a vector of N lanes, casts int16\u2192int32, then performs LOG_N butterfly stages with tl.gather peer exchange (peer at lane XOR step) and value = (lane&step)==0 ? value+peer : peer\u2212value, i.e. a fast WHT in natural (Sylvester/Hadamard) order.",
      "After the butterfly it permutes coefficients: reversed_lane = bit-reverse of lane over LOG_N bits (built via a static_range loop), then source_lane = reversed_lane ^ (reversed_lane >> 1) \u2014 a Gray-code step applied AFTER bit reversal \u2014 and stores result[lane] = value[source_lane] via tl.gather (lines 22\u201328).",
      "run() validates inputs, allocates a fresh int32 [B,N] output on x.device, launches a grid of (batch,) with N and LOG_N = bit_length\u22121 as constexprs, num_warps=4.",
      "No overflow risk inside the kernel: all arithmetic is int32 and intermediate/ final magnitudes are bounded by N*32768 <= 4194304 < 2^31, matching the contract's exactness argument.",
      "validate_inputs enforces the full public domain (dtype, shape, contiguity, view flags, inference mode, CUDA) and reference()/make_inputs() exist as described in the problem.",
      "Assumes N is a power of two so tl.arange(0,N) and the bit-level reorder are well defined \u2014 guaranteed by the domain (32/64/128)."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Does the reference's sign-change counting/sorting ever break the tie assumption ('counts are all distinct') for N in {32,64,128} \u2014 i.e., is the sequency ordering well-defined for all domain sizes?",
      "Is there any subtlety in tl.gather axis semantics or static_range constant-folding for LOG_N=6/7 that differs from the apparent intent on Triton 3.4.0?",
      "Does the peer\u2212value vs value\u2212peeer butterfly direction combined with the bit-reverse-then-Gray permutation produce the ascending-sequency order (Gray(w) direction), and is the two conventions' mismatch (kernel vs reference) a real failure?"
    ],
    "risk_map": [
      "Sequency\u2194natural index permutation: the standard mapping is natural = bitreverse(gray(sequency)) (Gray code applied first, then bit reversal), but the kernel computes source_lane = gray(bitreverse(lane)) (bit reversal first, then Gray). These two orderings of the operators generally differ, so the permutation may produce wrong coefficient order \u2014 a prime candidate for exact-comparison failure even when the butterfly values are correct.",
      "Butterfly sign convention: value = peer \u2212 value for lanes with the step bit set; the sign direction interacts with the permutation and could invert sequency order (descending vs ascending) or permute coefficients among rows.",
      "tl.gather semantics on a 1-D vector with axis=0 and shuffled indices \u2014 verify it performs the intended per-lane permutation of the value vector in Triton 3.4.0.",
      "Bit-reversal loop uses int32 shifts/or over LOG_N bits; confirm no sign-extension or width issue at N=128 (LOG_N=7), and that reversed_lane >> 1 is a logical shift of a nonnegative value.",
      "validate_inputs checks x.is_neg()/x.is_conj()/is_inference()/is_contiguous() \u2014 confirm these match contract semantics and don't wrongly reject legal inputs (e.g. contiguous views with nonzero storage offsets).",
      "Reference path itself: torch.argsort(changes) is stable and counts are distinct per contract, so reference is well-defined; kernel must match it exactly for all N in {32,64,128} and the full int16 range, including \u221232768."
    ],
    "scope_notes": [
      "Domain is limited to N in {32,64,128} and B in [1,128]; non-power-of-two or larger N are out of scope.",
      "Exact integer equality (atol=0, rtol=0) is the decision metric; approximations or normalized variants fail. Downstream-softmax-style tolerance arguments do not apply.",
